for publication somewhere.  Please criticize it constructively.

Ecoscam: The False Prophets of Ecological Apocalypse

by Ronald Bailey

reviewed by Tim Starr

In Ecoscam Ronald Bailey does a good job of putting claims of imminent
planetwide catastrophe in the context of the scientific evidence, biographical
information about the foremost of those who make them, and the agenda
which they all too often serve.  In so doing, Bailey becomes the latest
member of a species that happily isn't endangered, but growing in number:
the skeptical critic of apocalyptic environmentalism.

This is all done uncannily in the style of the PBS documentaries that usually
show nothing but full support for apocalyptics.  It even reads like the script
for such a show.  Reading it, one easily imagines a voiceover being done by a
journalist stating the words with the modulation and inflection of a TV
announcer - perhaps even with a BBC accent.  The author credit on the jacket
says that Bailey produces a PBS series called "Technopolitics," so that must be
where he learned how to produce this effect.  It doesn't read like an attempt to
make it sound authoritative, though.  It gives the impression that this is
simply how an experienced professional journalist gets the message across, which
he does quite well.

And the content of the message is dynamite: the first apocalyptics were
frightened of the threat of nuclear war, and included some of the Manhattan
Project scientists that brought "the bomb" into existence in the first
place.  They formed a broad coalition with activists whose political agendas
tended towards civil rights and pacifism, and were quite effective at
promoting predictions of the inevitability of nuclear war with the Soviets
and mobilizing support for nuclear disarmament.  Nuclear war turned out not
to be inevitable, however, and disarmament has been much faster after the
Soviet Union has collapsed than before.  In the process of supporting
disarmament, however, this coalition resorted to scare tactics, false
predictions and tenuous theoretical speculation (such as Carl Sagan's "Nuclear
Winter" scenario) in the service of their agenda - all too often at the expense
of the balance and objectivity that is expected from scientists.  This set the
apocalyptic pattern; many have followed in their footsteps.

Many catastrophes and crises have been predicted that turned out false: the
depletion of our resources was predicted by the Club of Rome in "The Limits
To Growth," mass starvation by the 1980s was predicted by Paul Ehrlich in
"The Population Bomb," etc.

These were false because they were chronically guilty of intellectual
malpractice.  The apocalyptics emphasized destabilizing feedback loops and
ignored stabilizing ones.  They neglected evidence that tended to refute
their claims.  They relied on theories which had one cause for the effects
they studied, when there were many co-factors.  And, somehow, the culprit
always was allegedly industrial capitalism, which had to be scrapped in
favor of global equality of condition.  Attempts to solve problems without
doing this were dismissed as treatments of only the symptoms of an
alleged disease that ignored its supposed root causes.  Certainly this 
description fits some better than others, the purpose is to bring the reader's
attention to these malpractices and their dangers, not convict anyone of them.

Apocalyptics responded with great hostility to their critics, calling them
"cornucopians" and "Pollyannas" - the classic ad hominem fallacy advised by
Cicero: "When you have no case, abuse the plaintiff."  Critics were even
accused of harming efforts to avert catastrophe by expressing their doubts in
public.  Scientific dissent was treated much like political dissent in any
totalitarian state.

In order to avert uncertain threats, proposals to establish political controls
with certainly high costs were, again, all too often made.  One group proposed
a UN Ecological Security Council with nigh-absolute power, and others made
similar suggestions.

These mistakes are being repeated in the present because the lessons of this
recent history haven't been learned.

The true virtue of this book is how much Bailey has to say that will be
unfamiliar even to many who've followed these controversies.  Famous names
such as Julian Simon, little-known scientific findings, and dirt on activists
like Jeremy Rifkin all make their way in, and make Ecoscam comprehensive.

For example, Bailey cites study that has found most of the global warming that
has already happened has only consisted of an increase in the average nightly
emperature due to increased cloudiness.  This confirms my experience living
in Berkeley, where the coldest nights are clear ones.  Clouds act as an
insulator of heat, keeping it in at night.  The implications for agriculture
are good: crop growing seasons start after the last frost of spring and end
at the first of fall.  If nights get warmer, then the season starts earlier
and ends later, making it longer.  Longer growing seasons mean more crops.
More crop supply means less price, according to the law of supply and demand,
and if fruits and vegetables are thus more affordable to the poor, they'll
eat more of them, making their diet healthier and reducing their risk of
getting cancer and other illnesses.  Thus, global warming may not be a
catastrophe at all, but quite a boon for the poor!

Rifkin, it turns out, comes from an activist background with organizations
that were openly communist in the late 1960s and early 1970s.  His Foundation
on Economic Trends used to be a front for the ...  If the farmers that he's
been trying to get to stop supporting the National Dairy only knew!

Bailey makes the good point that it was unnecessary to transform political
or economic systems to phase out CFCs, but is too hasty in presenting the
phase-out as acceptable.  He doesn't cover the costs and risks of this in
any detail at all.

He also uses excessively critical language to refer to his subjects.  He
seems to have let contempt for them slip out without having already provided
the reader with the basis for them.  "Vacuous," "doomsayers," and other
such specimens are sometimes unwarranted by the context.

These are minor imperfections in an otherwise very worthwhile book, however,
and a very necessary one that will hopefully help to rid us of catastrophists
guilty of intellectual malpractice by exposing it to the light of day - the
best disinfectant.  Then we'll be at less risk of getting conned into
destroying Western civilization without a better alternative.  It would
also help if Bailey's work and that of those like them taught these false
prophets the lesson of "The Boy Who Cried Wolf."  Each time they make scientific
predictions that turn out false, they diminish the credibility of true ones.
It would be a tragic irony if they desensitized the public to impending
disaster so much that we get completely taken by surprise, unprepared and
unready, for the real thing.

